  "title": "",
  "body": " Solve the following system of equations using substitution:     We need to start by solving one of the equations for one of the variables. It doesn't matter which equation we pick, and it doesn't matter which variable we pick. Since we solved for in the example above, let's solve for in this case. The first equation does not have a number multiplied by , so let's use that one:   So, we are going to plug in for in the second equation:   And now we can solve for :   Now that we've figured out that , we need to plug back into an equation to find . We could use either of the starting equations, but in this case, it will be easiest to use the equation we already solved for :   Therefore, our answer is .  Let's check our work using the strategies in the paragraph above. First, let's try plugging into the other starting equation, and make sure we get the same value for :   That worked! So, we can be pretty confident that we have the right answer. If we want to be really sure, let's plug our point into both starting equations and make sure they both work:    It works for both equations! Therefore, we can be very, very confident that we did indeed compute the right answer.    "
